WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
The primary assessment decision is difference or disorder.

2
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.240
A pattern unlike Mainstream American English is not automatically a disorder.

3
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:13.240
Learn the communication expectations of the client’s community, languages and dialects.

4
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.700
Limited eye contact, for example, may reflect a cultural expectation.

5
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.180
Interpret the observation before assigning a clinical label.

6
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:27.040
The cultural competence model is ASKED: awareness, skill, knowledge, encounters and desire.

7
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.400
Competence requires continuing self-assessment.

8
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.900
A language history asks which languages are

9
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.480
used, how frequently, where and with whom.

10
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.560
Assess abilities in both languages, one language at

11
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.700
a time, with appropriate assistance when needed.

12
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.000
Family, teachers and interpreters contribute information that

13
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.420
a single English test cannot supply.

14
00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.640
Dynamic assessment compares an independent attempt with performance after instruction and support.

15
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.440
Observe what cues help, how much support

16
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.120
is needed, and whether learning transfers.

17
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.020
Improvement can support an experience-based explanation.

18
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.020
Limited improvement raises concern, but the whole profile still matters.

19
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.860
Dynamic evidence is not simply a second administration of an unchanged test.

20
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.400
Brief the interpreter on the purpose and tasks,

21
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.400
work together during the assessment, and debrief afterward.

22
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.580
Speak to the client and check understanding.

23
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.340
Translating an English standardized test does not

24
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.940
create validated norms for another language.

25
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.660
Modified tasks can provide useful descriptive information; identify those limits

26
00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.322
in the report instead of presenting an invalid normative comparison.
